#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf01827dc23a26ed9d8d81333d735021d3a4eb494a434ba9fc38f1d110dfcc25.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <        \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <    \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](images/0a/0af9e8b587bbeb640ff9ec0cdc8197a9d99e8f5282772561b92ba74915cf1226.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924" :question/problem "![](images/38/38057eabc400c888e287be8dbbd8b4de172b625fa5922188f99f71112213ef3b.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty, 2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\{ x : |  x- x_0| <           \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $( x_0-\\delta,  x_0+\\delta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e3aa5b8e0e93f7fda306a6f39c999af9a587614795316ce12fff8da2c9c2dd2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/38/389aa307a3c0c10b5d51b86bed6e05331fb9299379652f06e65a4a7b9f776ead.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0992788a0ac7ce8cab2caabb41479ba2267e532412d1ef2c7115f56afb911c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                   \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <               \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=4-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8e/8e8e3296c59277b92eddddd3deacd7149fd9d7062aa05f4858e1846c3fb3238b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/07/077daf974625dd03a02b3f1c55e7b60c5a8c9686bc5515d78246bf7e1048e4cf.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ satisfy the equation $y = 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y > 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                  \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <              \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70dec41d9d5d24d82d1efafd65f1898ffcd5794dca98ca46d486cd3be428a18.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1, 1)$ and $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/3e/3e99fed83e9e31f223bfb4c1a9df87aa0b54e7865c1bfbc5b175b684c7c3c58a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ satisfy the equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ Therefore, the point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ea8503-592e-5da9-bf94-dbf1c1894619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238691} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3cb585-f127-5930-9e3b-7bcca744902f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238718} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239510} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eef8cf-fdfa-56b9-be1c-3b3a679c443b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239949} {:answer/id #uuid "e723e2bb-2b91-51b6-b191-0ff5b0c1e575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239510} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b2343f-c078-5f49-8bf2-efe62f09b44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2c7d13-747e-5ac0-bf88-b6f7f16d61aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105334" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/a1/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/02/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec8d07b-f074-551e-87b0-be8d07f75c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275604} {:answer/id #uuid "12ef0e3f-940d-54a6-b879-056c7271a92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275648} {:answer/id #uuid "14e3062f-1867-5b5c-b9bf-f133948f5e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275673} {:answer/id #uuid "46146491-4c02-583c-b4da-26afd46b9771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276095} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6fb0c4-71eb-59a9-a329-b326663c00e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec8d07b-f074-551e-87b0-be8d07f75c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275604} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf8aa29-3ce7-5aee-95bd-b1518a40ff52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14446fa2-dc95-59c6-9ff9-236caf0a3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-158562" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9f1302-a225-50dc-8282-9cdc30e460cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276298} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5a4698-2fec-5e90-91df-84219743e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276727} {:answer/id #uuid "92236399-f0b6-5795-b074-da5f2cda69c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276800} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc48f93-0f2e-5c19-8450-cae78ea3b874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8af4d1-9fe9-507f-bcdf-572cdb0ebc23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2253cfff-ee0a-5c15-93af-d26f561ec17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158603" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} {:answer/id #uuid "4140e559-6092-5ae2-8bcd-a98f2357d749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276050} {:answer/id #uuid "42bb2cbf-5462-514b-b822-00b92a2500dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276065} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4265f7-2803-5a4c-af0b-23d6cfcd9d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277152} {:answer/id #uuid "e710bfdf-fe8b-54d4-91db-e06b6c274c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ce3a08-8352-57fb-9450-c1e8717cb404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d4676b-36dc-5235-80c5-02c2a6d333fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158733" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1e10761-2095-5c5f-bdb6-a44a31e70177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121452} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f23729-ccf4-5d2b-bae7-5e3e14c2dce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121453} {:answer/id #uuid "ed12a047-62f9-504d-8832-589427d42283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121454} {:answer/id #uuid "2268d166-ab50-5896-8205-9550937dbc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121455} {:answer/id #uuid "47b46fed-767a-5aaf-925b-addab2cdbd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121456}] :answer-field/id #uuid "02ab6e58-4274-5937-87a2-57b1757d8b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121451}] :question/id #uuid "b288f6b3-b69c-5d49-b36c-166c5ccca7a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159266" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ccbc1cf370686432f144f9bf3f8f389b5ee1beb652b704266708fc1e9d8e6d.png)\n\nConsider the set $M = {0}∪(2, ∞)$ and the points $A$, $B$, and $C$, shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n2. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n3. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048011b9-ffee-53bf-ac13-2e97743e225c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275519} {:answer/id #uuid "0efa31d6-31db-55c2-9b88-0a9a3a3453fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275607} {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276052} {:answer/id #uuid "4305747f-a88f-529f-a758-988093ddc9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276069} {:answer/id #uuid "eda63547-3ece-56ee-9327-e038a1f457a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276052} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bae57d-74be-5ed9-b597-76e04905579c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e85ebcb1-0815-545b-a7ea-7afc898a561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159502" :question/problem "![](images/79/79fc33355e32fc3b57a129e83fdf35ecdd1e5b0f3275c433a49d85642cf40f3f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a7/a7f99060032c4f79df8b2ee68e220f31bb14f29f99e9190c9b385d073f3274d9.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "696e584f-f454-593e-bd58-abb69e57b3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377650} {:answer/id #uuid "a3452674-e7ae-5e7f-858b-9637d1e75a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377651} {:answer/id #uuid "a978c7d2-6987-586e-9304-4ae71f2c36a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377652} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c0e42c-4f23-58b3-8d95-773d2a6c79d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377653} {:answer/id #uuid "a27fb0f0-ebd3-5673-9103-2976012540f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27fb0f0-ebd3-5673-9103-2976012540f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377654} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a35593-4c9e-5037-b87f-d4e48a3bf713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10cee0d6-c023-521a-a779-cab7a62d3b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64999" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7ffcda47f0d750ce9f25e50b0b28091ff4279467313fbe604eac0fc9b1229806.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/08/080d6cf1cd468b7ddfe65df2e6a21792e1afe470ddd571c6b5ec25569d1fb537.png)\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to \n$M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}]}
